(borge-bug (id "BORGE-1790987438")
  (title "single-row tree_generations makes every branch-free data.* write contend on one row; one stalled transaction freezes all datum writes tree-wide")
  (status open)
  (created 2026-10-02)
  (filed-by roerick)
  (doc (|FOUND 2026-10-02 during the prod lock pileup (BORGE-1790987430).
This design choice turned a single stalled writer into a tree-wide write
freeze.

migrations/018_tree_versions.sql defines the TREE-WIDE head counter:

    CREATE TABLE tree_generations ( generation bigint NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 );
    INSERT INTO tree_generations (generation) SELECT 0 WHERE NOT EXISTS (...);

There is exactly ONE row, with no branch key. For every branch-free data.*
path, the tree_version_append trigger (migrations/019, 020, 021, 025) runs:

    UPDATE tree_generations SET generation = generation + 1
    RETURNING generation INTO ngen;

For branch-owned code paths the counter is per-branch
(branch_generations, keyed by branch_id), so those writes contend per branch.
But every data.* datum write — notes, todos, chat, any package data row —
takes a row lock on the single tree_generations row for the duration of its
transaction.

CONSEQUENCE: any transaction that writes a datum and then stalls before
COMMIT holds the tree_generations row lock, and EVERY other datum writer
tree-wide blocks on it. In the 2026-10-02 incident the leaked reader (pid 572,
idle in transaction) plus a chain of UPDATE tree_nodes writers serialized all
data writes. This is the amplifier that made one abandoned transaction take
down the whole habitat.

SPEC: borge/one-tree.borg / borge/tree-time.borg (tree_generations head
counter) — the single-writer global counter was chosen for simplicity.

FIX DIRECTION (choose after measuring write volume):
  - Key the counter by a shard: e.g. tree_generations(scope text PRIMARY KEY)
    with a shard derived from the datum path/id (or per-type_name), so
    unrelated writes no longer contend. Generation numbers become per-shard;
    readers that assume a single monotonic gen would need to chain/min.
  - OR use a sequence / gapless-per-scope scheme that does not take a
    transaction-scoped row lock on a hot single row.
  - OR (weaker) keep one row but ensure datum writes never hold it across
    user-code / blocking work — reduces, does not eliminate, contention.
NOTE: the counter is exactly-once-sensitive (tree/versions gen windows,
undo), so a sharded redesign must preserve per-path ordering.
VERIFY: concurrent datum writes from many fibers on disjoint paths do not
serialize on one row (pg_locks shows no common single-row waiter), and
version/gen semantics + undo still hold.
|))
)
